questions.prompt:
Prove that the one-body operator $\hat T=\sum_{
u\mu}T_{
u\mu}c^\dagger_
u c_\mu$ holds for fermions (Eq. 1.61), by repeating the boson argument of Eqs. (1.58)-(1.60) (Book Exercise 1.1).

questions.solution:
Book Exercise 1.1. Act with $\hat T_{tot}=\sum_j T_j$ on $|
u_1\cdots
u_Nangle=c^\dagger_{
u_1}\cdots c^\dagger_{
u_N}|0angle$. Each term $T_j$ replaces the operator in position $j$, so
$$\hat T|
u_1\cdots
u_Nangle=\sum_j\sum_{ab}T_{ab}\,\delta_{a,
u_j}\,c^\dagger_{
u_1}\cdots\underbrace{c^\dagger_b}_{	ext{position }j}\cdots c^\dagger_{
u_N}|0angle,$$
with $T_{ab}=\int dr\,\psi^*_b T\psi_a$ as in Eq. (1.27b).

Sign count for fermions. Writing the replacement as an operator, $c_a$ must first move past the $j-1$ operators in front of position $j$, giving $(-1)^{j-1}$. Then $c^\dagger_b$ must move back to position $j$, giving another $(-1)^{j-1}$. The two signs cancel, so each occurrence gives the same term with a plus sign.

Summing over $j$ (the occupied states $a$ that equal $
u_j$) gives $\sum_{ab}T_{ab}c^\dagger_b c_a$ acting on the state, which is the operator form. Relabeling indices gives $\hat T=\sum_{
u\mu}T_{
u\mu}c^\dagger_
u c_\mu$, Eq. (1.61).
Result: the one-body operator has the same form for fermions as for bosons, because the sign from the anticommutators cancels.
